> That's good but I think it reveals another principle: LLM needs to
> discern inference and facts. When it makes claims, it should look for
> opportunities to verify it, i.e. uncover and report a fact. If a claim
> is inference, that should be noted as well.
>
> In other words, claims should be clearly indicated as facts or
> inference, with evidence to back up each. Is that a reasonable
> instruction that will result in better outcomes? I'm interested to see
> if there's any prior art on that sort of thing.

Yes, with two adjustments, and there is solid prior art.

The first adjustment is to ask for evidence, not labels. A tag such
as "verified" is model output like any other, and the model can
write it wrongly, the same failure "Own the loop" describes for
control tokens. A command and its output, or a file and line, is
something you can re-run. So the rule should require the evidence,
and treat a bare label as worth nothing.

The second is to label only what matters. Tagging every sentence
adds noise, and readers stop reading the tags. Mark the claims a
decision rests on, and every claim not checked. A fact shown with
its evidence needs no tag.

On whether it gives better outcomes: I have no measurement for this
list, so that part is inference. The research below shows that
checking claims after drafting cuts hallucinated facts, and the
"look for a way to verify" half of your rule is that step. The pkg
mistake fits it exactly; one command would have turned the wrong
inference into the right fact.

Prior art. I checked the first three with a web search this pass,
reading the search results and summaries, not the full documents:

- US intelligence analysis, ICD 203 "Analytic Standards" (ODNI).
  Tradecraft standard 3 reads "Properly distinguishes between
  underlying intelligence information and analysts' assumptions and
  judgments". It also asks analysts to state the assumptions an
  argument hinges on. This is your rule nearly word for word.
- Anthropic's "Reduce hallucinations" guide in the Claude docs. It
  recommends allowing "I don't know", grounding answers in direct
  quotes, and having the model find a supporting quote for each
  claim and retract any it cannot support.
- "Chain-of-Verification Reduces Hallucination in Large Language
  Models" (Dhuliawala et al., Meta, ACL Findings 2024). The model
  drafts, plans questions to check its draft, answers them
  independently, and revises. It measured fewer hallucinations on
  list questions, closed-book QA, and long-form text.

From memory, not checked this pass:

- The Linux kernel's Tested-by trailer, and the habit of writing
  "compile-tested only" under the --- line. Both say what was run,
  not just what is believed to work.
- Evidentiality in linguistics. Some languages, Turkish and Quechua
  among them, mark in the grammar whether the speaker saw a thing or
  inferred or heard it.

I have sent the rule as "[PATCH] claude: Mark claims as fact or
inference" [2]. It adds a section to CLAUDE.md, after "Citing
messages", with both adjustments above and the notmuch-emacs mistake
